Unlock with the AIDL Gatekeeper and other protectors (16.0 version).

On turner (HyperOS 3, Android 16) TWRP failed every pattern: the active
protector uses Weaver (android.hardware.weaver-service.nxp on the NXP eSE,
reached through OMAPI, which recovery cannot run), and the phone also keeps
a Gatekeeper protector for the same synthetic password.

What changed vs the 14.1 patch: the AIDL Gatekeeper plumbing is already
upstream in twrp-16.0 (Gatekeeper_Verify tries AIDL first, then HIDL, and
android.hardware.gatekeeper-V1-ndk is already linked), so this patch only
carries the turner-specific logic:
- unwrapSyntheticPasswordBlob used the ACTIVE sp-handle for the keystore
  alias; use the handle of the protector we are actually trying.
- Decrypt_User_Synth_Pass takes an optional handle_str so the fallback
  below can retry with a different protector.
- Decrypt_User_Other_Protectors tries every protector that has Gatekeeper
  data (.secdis) when the active one fails.
Applies to TWRP-Test android_system_vold twrp-16.0.

diff --git a/Decrypt.cpp b/Decrypt.cpp
--- a/Decrypt.cpp
+++ b/Decrypt.cpp
@@ -570,9 +570,11 @@
     ::keystore::hidl_vec<uint8_t> iv_hidlvec;
     iv_hidlvec.setToExternal(byteptr, 12);  // The IV is the first 12 bytes of the spblob
 
+    // Decrypt the protector we are actually trying (handle_str), not the active
+    // sp-handle: a Weaver protector can be active while we fall back to this
+    // Gatekeeper one (Decrypt_User_Other_Protectors)
     KeystoreInfo keystore_info;
-    std::string handle = keystore_info.getHandle(user_id);
-    std::string keystore_alias = keystore_info.getAlias(handle);
+    std::string keystore_alias = keystore_info.getAlias(handle_str);
     unsigned char* cipher_text = byteptr + 12;  // The cipher text comes immediately after the IV
     ::keystore::hidl_vec<uint8_t> cipher_text_hidlvec;
 
@@ -855,7 +857,7 @@
 }
 
 
-bool Decrypt_User_Synth_Pass(const userid_t user_id, const std::string& Password) {
+bool Decrypt_User_Synth_Pass(const userid_t user_id, const std::string& Password, std::string handle_str = "") {
     printf("Attempting to unlock the user's LSKF-based SP protector\n");
     bool retval = false;
     void* weaver_key = nullptr;
@@ -871,7 +873,8 @@
     std::string spblob_path = spblob_path_char;
 
     KeystoreInfo keystore_info;
-    std::string handle_str = keystore_info.getHandle(user_id);
+    if (handle_str.empty())
+        handle_str = keystore_info.getHandle(user_id);
     // The synthetic password key is looked up by alias, so keystore2 needs the
     // installed system's database before any of the calls below.
     if (!android::keystore::syncKeystoreDb()) {
@@ -1038,6 +1041,33 @@
     return 0;
 }
 
+// HyperOS keeps the LSKF in a Weaver protector backed by the NXP eSE, reached through OMAPI,
+// which recovery cannot run. It also keeps a Gatekeeper protector for the same synthetic password,
+// so try every protector that has Gatekeeper data (.secdis) when the active one fails.
+static bool Decrypt_User_Other_Protectors(const userid_t user_id, const std::string& Password) {
+    const std::string dir = "/data/system_de/" + std::to_string(user_id) + "/spblob/";
+    DIR* d = opendir(dir.c_str());
+    if (!d)
+        return false;
+    const std::string suffix = ".secdis";
+    bool ret = false;
+    while (dirent* de = readdir(d)) {
+        std::string id = de->d_name;
+        if (id.size() <= suffix.size() || id.compare(id.size() - suffix.size(), suffix.size(), suffix) != 0)
+            continue;
+        id.resize(id.size() - suffix.size());
+        // file names are %016x, keystore aliases use %x
+        id.erase(0, std::min(id.find_first_not_of('0'), id.size() - 1));
+        printf("Trying protector '%s'\n", id.c_str());
+        if (Decrypt_User_Synth_Pass(user_id, Password, id)) {
+            ret = true;
+            break;
+        }
+    }
+    closedir(d);
+    return ret;
+}
+
